<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of Cuba</h1><p class="st-meta">1906 CE – 1909 CE · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second US occupation of Cuba, from 1906 to 1909, invoked under the Platt Amendment after a revolt against President Estrada Palma.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Tomás Estrada Palma's</a> disputed re-election in 1906 provoked a Liberal revolt in August. Estrada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> asked for US help and then resigned, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> without a government. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> sent Secretary of War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a>, who proclaimed a provisional government in September 1906.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">Taft</a> was soon replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-magoon">Charles Magoon</a>, who governed until 1909 while preserving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba's</a> flag and laws. Magoon used government jobs and contracts to buy peace among Cuban factions, built roads and public works, created a permanent army and drafted new electoral laws. Critics accused his administration of waste and of draining the surplus left by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Estrada Palma</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Elections in 1908 were won by the Liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-gomez">José Miguel Gómez</a>, who took office on 28 January 1909, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba</a> (1902-1959). The occupation confirmed US influence over Cuban politics for decades.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-government-of-cuba.jpg" alt="Provisional Government of Cuba" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Jacobolus assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1896%E2%80%931908).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1906 CE – 1909 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Howard Taft, Charles Magoon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Second occupation of Cuba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1908&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-cuba" aria-label="Provisional Government of Cuba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2481 1324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Provisional Government of Cuba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2801 1580L2570 1008M2801 1580L2801 1580"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2801 1580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Havana. <a href="/atlas?year=1908&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-cuba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
